      explain: "Evaluate on three axes. **Task success**: did the end state match the goal (order cancelled, form submitted correctly), checked by code against the system of record. **Efficiency and cost**: steps, time, tokens, and images or audio minutes per successful task. **Experience**: for voice, latency percentiles (p50, p95, p99), interruptions, and recovery from misunderstandings; for UI agents, how often a human had to intervene. Build a task suite from real workflows with deterministic setup (seeded accounts, fixed pages), run each task several times because agents are stochastic, and report success rate with its spread. Use **percentiles** for latency: sort the measurements and take the value at the right rank.",
      diagram: "task suite (seeded env) ─▶ run each task ×5 ─▶ state check (pass/fail) ─▶ success rate ± spread\nper turn latency samples ─▶ sort ─▶ p50, p95, p99 (nearest rank)\nreport: success · steps · cost · p95 latency · human interventions",
      ex1: ['text', 'percentiles hide or reveal problems', "latencies (ms): 80 90 95 100 105 110 120 130 300 2000\nmean ≈ 313 ms    p50 = 105 ms    p95 = 2000 ms  ← the slow tail users remember"],
      ex2: ['text', 'a state-checked task', "task:   cancel order ord_1042 via the web portal\nsetup:  seeded account with order ord_1042 (status: processing)\ncheck:  SELECT status FROM orders WHERE id = 'ord_1042' → 'cancelled'\n        and no other orders changed"],
      deepDive: {
        explain: "End-state checks must also assert that **nothing else changed**: an agent that cancels the right order and two wrong ones should fail. Seed environments deterministically and reset them between runs; otherwise results drift. Agents are stochastic, so run several trials and report pass@1 (single-attempt success) and variability; τ-bench introduced pass^k (succeeding on all k trials) to measure consistency, which matters for production reliability. For latency, use nearest-rank or interpolated percentiles consistently and collect enough samples (p99 needs hundreds of turns to be meaningful). Combine automated suites with sampled human review of recordings for qualities metrics miss: tone, clarity, and whether users felt understood.",
        example: ['js', 'nearest-rank percentile', "function percentile(values, p) {\n  if (!values.length) return null;\n  const sorted = [...values].sort((a, b) => a - b);\n  const rank = Math.ceil(p / 100 * sorted.length);       // nearest-rank method\n  return sorted[Math.max(0, rank - 1)];\n}\npercentile([120, 80, 300, 95, 110, 2000, 105, 90, 100, 130], 95); // 2000"],
        tradeoffs: [['Metric', 'Captures', 'Misses'], ['End-state success', 'Correct outcomes', 'Wasteful paths, tone'], ['pass^k consistency', 'Reliability across runs', 'Needs k runs per task'], ['Latency percentiles', 'Tail experience', 'Quality of content'], ['Human review samples', 'Tone, clarity, trust', 'Scale; consistency']],
        failures: [['High success but unhappy users', 'Only end-state metrics', 'Add latency percentiles and human review'], ['Results differ every run', 'Non-deterministic environment setup', 'Seed and reset environments'], ['Agent passes while damaging other data', 'Checks only the target state', 'Assert no unintended changes'], ['Mean latency looks fine, callers complain', 'Averages hide the tail', 'Report p95 and p99']],
      },
